# Goblin kit — the POLYGON half of the goblin, authored in WAM.
#
# WHY TWO LANGUAGES FOR ONE CHARACTER. .blob builds flesh out of blended
# signed-distance primitives, and it is very good at exactly the things a mesh
# is bad at: everything merges by construction, nothing has a seam, and a limb
# is four numbers. It is correspondingly bad at anything HARD — smooth-min
# rounds every edge it touches, so a buckle, a rivet, a plate lip or a blade
# either smears or has to be carved back with a `hard` blend that fights the
# whole aesthetic. WAM is a text→glTF mesh compiler, which is the other half of
# that trade. So: flesh in .blob, plate in .wam.
#
# The polygon layer also sidesteps a problem the SDF path has not solved. All
# the flesh shares ONE material — per-part colour needs a data-texture row and
# a second weighted fold at every hit pixel to avoid Voronoi seams mid-limb.
# A separate mesh just has its own material, so steel on green flesh is free.
#
# THE SKELETON BELOW MIRRORS characters/goblin.blob AND MUST TRACK IT. Every
# bone, angle and length is the same joint in the same place; if the .blob
# skeleton moves and this does not, the plate floats. Two conversions apply
# and neither is optional:
#
#   * UNITS. WAM lengths are fractions of `height`; .blob's are metres. Every
#     number here is the .blob metre value / 1.30.
#   * PITCH SIGN ON `down` BONES. WAM's pitch tips an `up` bone forward and a
#     `down` bone BACKWARD; .blob's always carries toward +z. So `up` bones
#     (spine1, chest, neck, skull) share their pitch verbatim, and every `down`
#     bone (upperarm, forearm, thigh, shin) has it NEGATED. Getting this wrong
#     is what gave the .blob goblin kangaroo knees in the first place, and it
#     is silent — the model compiles either way and just wears its armour on
#     the wrong side of the limb. `tilt` needs no such flip: both languages
#     splay a `down` bone outward on +.
#
# RING SIZES ARE MEASURED, NOT EYEBALLED. Each piece wraps flesh that is not
# in this file, so every w/d below comes from marching the .blob field outward
# from the bone axis, per limb-isolated cluster, and converting to height
# fractions — the SDF answers "where is the surface" directly, which is the
# one thing a mesh pipeline has to raycast for. Measured flesh, for reference:
#
#   upperarm t0.00 w=0.085  t0.18 w=0.055  t0.42 w=0.051
#   forearm  w=0.045 all along
#   hips     t0.40 w=0.109 d=0.146   t0.62 w=0.126 d=0.174   t0.90 w=0.134 d=0.184
#   shin     w=0.055 all along
#
# Every ring here sits a few thousandths proud of those. Re-measure before
# retuning: the arm-clearance work already moved the torso's whole cross
# section once.
# BUILD:  scripts/build-wam-kit.sh goblin
#   -> public/assets/lab/goblin-kit.gltf, which the lab loads (KITS in
#      lab-main.ts). WAM lives outside this repo, so the compiled glTF is
#      committed and that script records how to regenerate it.
#
# THREE OF WAM'S LINTS ALWAYS FIRE ON A KIT AND ARE CORRECT TO IGNORE. They
# check a whole CHARACTER, and this is only the clothes: `spine1`/`neck`/`skull`
# have no geometry near them (that flesh lives in the .blob), and the lowest
# geometry floats 0.174 above the ground (there are no feet here). A warning
# about anything else is real.
model goblin-kit
  height 1.30
  # chunky = 8-sided lofts. Faceted plate against smooth raymarched flesh is
  # the contrast this experiment is for — if it reads as cheap rather than
  # deliberate, this is the first knob to try at `smooth`.
  style chunky

palette
  iron    #6e7378
  leather #4a3526
  brass   #9c7a38
  # Near-black, because a sunglass lens is almost all reflection — the COLOUR
  # barely matters and the shine is the whole read. kit-overlay.ts gives it the
  # lowest roughness and the highest env intensity of anything in the kit.
  # Dark waxed leather for the boots, and a cloth grey for the gaiter cuffs.
  boot    #2c2a28
  # Same steel as `iron`; a separate name so the spikes have their own fit pin and (Task 7) their own wear.
  plate   #6e7378
  webbing #3b3f42
  glass   #14161a
  # Glossy black plastic for the sunglass bridge and temple arms (LOOK `black` in kit-overlay.ts); its own
  # name because `band` is the watch strap and the test pins that to the left wrist.
  black   #17181a
  band    #1c1c20
  screen  #22282e

skeleton
  # `hips` HAS NO COUNTERPART IN goblin.blob, and leaving it out is a silent
  # 10.5 cm error. .blob's root is a BONE with a length — `root pelvis at 0.60
  # len=0.105` runs from the crotch up to the hip line, and everything that
  # says `parent=pelvis` there hangs off its TAIL. WAM's root is a bare POINT,
  # so parenting spine1 straight to it drops the entire trunk (and every piece
  # of plate on it) by that length. `hips` restores the missing segment, and
  # every .blob `parent=pelvis` becomes `parent=hips` here.
  #
  # It also carries the belt: `bones=pelvis..pelvis` is a zero-length path and
  # WAM rejects it outright, which is how this was found.
  root pelvis at 0.4615
  bone hips   parent=pelvis dir=up len=0.0808
  bone spine1 parent=hips dir=up pitch=13 len=0.1038
  bone chest  parent=spine1 dir=up pitch=9  len=0.1154
  bone neck   parent=chest  dir=up pitch=-17 len=0.1077
  bone skull  parent=neck   dir=up pitch=-5  len=0.1808
  mirror
    bone clavicle parent=chest    side=0.0277 dir=side len=0.0323
    # pitch NEGATED from .blob's -12 — see the header.
    bone upperarm parent=clavicle dir=down pitch=12  tilt=22 len=0.1808
    bone forearm  parent=upperarm dir=down pitch=-11 tilt=-5 len=0.1808
    bone hand     parent=forearm  dir=down len=0.0554
    bone thigh    parent=hips     side=0.0446 dir=down pitch=-9 tilt=5  len=0.2231
    bone shin     parent=thigh    dir=down pitch=11  tilt=-7 len=0.2077
    bone foot     parent=shin     dir=fwd len=0.1000
  end

parts
  # EVERY PIECE IS AN OPEN SHELL — `cap ... none` almost throughout, and that
  # is a correctness decision, not a shortcut. A `cap flat` on a tube that
  # wraps a limb puts a disc THROUGH the limb, with a vertex dead on the bone
  # axis: the belt's cap centre sat 61 mm inside the goblin, the greaves' 35 mm.
  # Hidden while the flesh happens to be thick enough to swallow them, and a
  # plate poking out of a shin the moment it is not. Real plate is a shell
  # anyway, so the geometry is also more honest. Rendered double-sided by
  # kit-overlay.ts, which is what an open shell needs.
  #
  # It has a second payoff: with no interior geometry, EVERY vertex is on the
  # outer surface, so "no vertex may sit inside the flesh" becomes an exact
  # test rather than one that has to guess which vertices are hidden. That is
  # goblin-kit.test.ts, and it reads the compiled mesh, not this text.
  mirror
    # PAULDRON — football-pad shoulders (owner, 2026-10-02, from the reference photo:
    # big bulbous riveted metal shoulder pads). Rebuilt for the phase-1 body.
    #
    # THE BODY CHANGED UNDER THE OLD ONE. .blob's shoulder is now a round of r 0.0405 m
    # (measured, measure.md; it was 0.054) centred on the clavicle tail at
    # (0.078, 0.985, 0.054) m, and the arm axis is (0.366, -0.906, -0.209) (from the
    # upperarm t0 -> t0.25 samples). The pad is a SHELL around that sphere, the same
    # technique as before (a bone loft cannot start at t<0, so it is a free ray aimed
    # along the arm, with each ring set from the shell's own profile), but BULBOUS on
    # purpose: R = 0.060 m, 19.5 mm over the orb, where the old plate hugged it to 8 mm.
    # That is the football-pad read.
    #
    # PROFILE. s is metres along the arm axis from the orb centre (negative = up toward
    # the neck). Above the equator the rings follow the sphere, rho = sqrt(R^2 - s^2).
    # Below it they follow an ellipse, rho = R sqrt(1 - (s/0.105)^2), tapering onto the
    # arm, and the last ring flares to a lip (0.043) so the rim reads as an edge. The arm
    # flesh at the rim is r 0.030, so the lip stands ~13 mm off it. ring w/d = 2 rho / 1.30:
    #   s -0.054 rho .0262 | -0.040 .0447 | -0.020 .0566 | 0 .0600
    #   s +0.025 .0583     | +0.050 .0528 | +0.072 .0437 | +0.085 .0430 (lip)
    # The ray starts at s = -0.054: 0.054 m back along the arm from the orb centre, which is
    # offset (-0.01976, 0.04892, 0.01129) m = (-0.0152, 0.0376, 0.0087) of height. It runs
    # 0.139 m (0.1069 of height) to s +0.085, so ring t = (s + 0.054) / 0.139. `pitch=12
    # tilt=22` re-aims the ray down the same arm axis (WAM's pitch sign is .blob's negated).
    #
    # ITS INNER RIM IS STILL BURIED IN THE CHEST, forced as before: the shell is 120 mm
    # across and its centre is 78 mm from the body axis, so it reaches x 0.018 where the
    # chest wall is at ~0.056. Deepest vertex ~38 mm in, bounded in goblin-kit.test.ts.
    # BIGGER (owner, 2026-10-02, with the yoke: "slightly oversized"): R 0.060 -> 0.066, the
    # lower ellipse L 0.105 -> 0.115, lip at s +0.092. Profile now (s m, rho m):
    #   s -0.060 .0275 | -0.044 .0492 | -0.022 .0622 | 0 .0660
    #   s +0.026 .0643 | +0.052 .0589 | +0.078 .0485 | +0.092 .0470 (lip)
    # Ray from s -0.060: offset (-0.02196, 0.05436, 0.01254) m = (-0.0169, 0.0418, 0.0096) of
    # height, length 0.152 m = 0.1169, ring t = (s + 0.060) / 0.152. `side=0.005` moves every
    # ring 6.5 mm outward so the bigger shell does not tuck deeper into the chest.
    loft pauldron bone=upperarm at=0.0 offset=(-0.0169,0.0418,0.0096) dir=down pitch=12 tilt=22 len=0.1169 material=iron
      ring 0.000 w=0.0423 d=0.0423 side=0.005
      ring 0.105 w=0.0757 d=0.0757 side=0.005
      ring 0.250 w=0.0957 d=0.0957 side=0.005
      ring 0.395 w=0.1015 d=0.1015 side=0.005
      ring 0.566 w=0.0989 d=0.0989 side=0.005
      ring 0.737 w=0.0906 d=0.0906 side=0.005
      ring 0.908 w=0.0746 d=0.0746 side=0.005
      ring 1.000 w=0.0723 d=0.0723 side=0.005
      cap start=dome end=none

    # PAULDRON LAME — the second, overlapping plate under the pad (the reference's pads
    # are stacked), a flared band on the upper arm. Chain loft on the upperarm bone
    # (0.235 m): t 0.30 is s 0.0705 m from the orb centre, so it starts inside the pad's
    # last 15 mm and stands outside it (rho 0.047 vs the pad's 0.0437 there; coincident
    # surfaces would z-fight). Arm flesh r 0.0295 at t0.5, so the narrowest ring (0.040)
    # clears it by 10 mm. ring w/d = 2 rho / 1.30: rho .047, .050, .041.
    loft lame bones=upperarm..upperarm material=iron
      ring 0.30 w=0.0723 d=0.0723
      ring 0.38 w=0.0769 d=0.0769
      ring 0.52 w=0.0631 d=0.0631
      cap start=none end=none

    # BRACER — a leather cuff. Flesh is 0.045 the whole way down the forearm.
    loft bracer bones=forearm..forearm material=leather
      ring 0.34 w=0.060 d=0.060
      ring 0.58 w=0.066 d=0.066
      ring 0.84 w=0.062 d=0.062
      cap start=none end=none

    # BOOT SHAFT, CUFF AND FOOT — rebuilt 2026-10-02 against the phase-1 body
    # (owner: "the boots clip"). The old greave and boot were sized from a
    # table 10-30% fatter than the sinew goblin (measure.md in
    # docs/dev-notes/2026-10-02-goblin-armour/), so they stood proud of a
    # shin that had shrunk. Every ring below is the MEASURED flesh (metres,
    # marched from sdBody per limb cluster, see measure.md) divided by 1.30 and
    # then scaled by 1.19: 8% for `style chunky`'s facet midpoints sitting at
    # cos(22.5) = 92.4% of the nominal radius, and 10% so the smooth-min does
    # not bulge through between ring samples. Boots are worn UNDER the painted
    # trousers' hem, so the cuff just covers the paint seam.
    #
    # Shaft: the shin chain from t0.42 (y 0.31 m) to the ankle. Flesh w there:
    # 0.0585 m at t0.42, 0.0530 at t0.60, 0.0489 at t0.75 (the thinnest point),
    # and the ankle knob 0.0665 at t1.0.
    loft bootshaft bones=shin..shin material=boot
      ring 0.42 w=0.056 d=0.056
      ring 0.60 w=0.051 d=0.051
      ring 0.78 w=0.047 d=0.047
      ring 0.92 w=0.054 d=0.054
      ring 1.00 w=0.062 d=0.062
      cap start=none end=none

    # Cuff: a gaiter band that folds over the boot top, a little wider than the
    # shaft and flaring out at the top edge so it reads as cloth over leather.
    loft cuff bones=shin..shin material=webbing
      ring 0.34 w=0.062 d=0.062
      ring 0.40 w=0.066 d=0.066
      ring 0.50 w=0.059 d=0.059
      cap start=none end=none

    # Foot: a free ray along the foot bone's direction starting 0.040 m BEHIND
    # the ankle, because the flesh heel reaches z -0.037 m (ankle z -0.0062) and
    # a loft cannot start at t<0 of a bone. Rings are by z (metres, ankle at
    # -0.006): w is lateral, d is height (frame=up). Measured flesh width at
    # the ring's z: heel 0.0345, ankle knob 0.0665, arch 0.052, ball 0.072, toe
    # pad 0.074 (to z +0.146). The foot is a flat bar 0.04-0.05 m tall centred
    # on y 0.157.
    #   z -0.046 w .046 d .060 | -0.030 .062 .076 | -0.006 .080 .082
    #   z +0.020 .074 .076     | +0.050 .066 .066 | +0.085 .088 .064
    #   z +0.124 .092 .058     | +0.152 .070 .046 | +0.172 .040 .030 tip
    loft boot bone=shin at=1.0 offset=(0,0,-0.0306) dir=fwd len=0.1677 frame=up material=boot
      ring 0.00 w=0.0354 d=0.0462
      ring 0.07 w=0.0477 d=0.0585
      ring 0.18 w=0.0615 d=0.0631
      ring 0.30 w=0.0569 d=0.0585
      ring 0.44 w=0.0508 d=0.0508
      ring 0.60 w=0.0677 d=0.0492
      ring 0.78 w=0.0708 d=0.0446
      ring 0.91 w=0.0538 d=0.0354
      ring 1.00 w=0.0308 d=0.0231 tip
      cap start=dome end=point

    # KNEE PLATE — a squarish collar with the middle ring pushed forward so it
    # reads as a kneecap, over the bend: a chain loft across thigh..shin, which
    # WAM skins with blended weights at the joint (the ogre and clown kits do the
    # same), so it follows the knee instead of creasing. The chain is 0.29 +
    # 0.27 = 0.56 m, knee at t 0.518. Flesh w: 0.0700 m at thigh t0.9, 0.0774 at
    # the knee, 0.066 at shin t0.15.
    loft kneeplate bones=thigh..shin material=iron shape=squarish
      ring 0.44 w=0.064 d=0.064
      ring 0.52 w=0.076 d=0.080 fwd=0.006
      ring 0.59 w=0.066 d=0.066
      cap start=none end=none
  end

  # PAULDRON SPIKES — LEFT PAD ONLY (owner, 2026-10-02: "kind of curious in adding some spikes
  # on one or both of the pauldrons"). One side on purpose: it is a mismatched, scavenged look,
  # and it is easy to mirror if the owner wants both (move these into the `mirror` block and
  # use `on=pauldron`). They are outside the mirror block like the watch, pinned to upperarm.l.
  #
  # `on=pauldron.l` snaps each base to the pad's surface and sinks it in, so the spikes are flush
  # by construction (WAM SPEC, "on="). The offsets are only rough aims, as that section says:
  # points on the R 0.066 dome about the orb centre, in height fractions (metres / 1.30), toward
  # (0.45, 0.86, 0) for the tall one and (0.50, 0.72, +/-0.47) for the front and back ones.
  # `dir=side` starts each outward and `up=` bends it skyward, which gives a curved horn-like
  # spike and sidesteps WAM's sweep taking no outward tilt of its own. Radii are height
  # fractions: base ~21 mm radius on the tall one, tall spike, 0.075 f long, flankers 0.056 f. First pass was r 0.011/0.007 with up=35 per segment and rendered as thin flat blades; the second is chunkier and bends further up (55 + 40 degrees) so the tips stand upright like spikes.
  # `plate` (not `iron`) so goblin-kit.test.ts's plate-fit pin does not have to cover the tips.
  sweep spike_a bone=upperarm.l at=0.0 offset=(0.0235,0.045,0) dir=side on=pauldron.l material=plate
    seg len=0.030 r=0.016 up=55
    seg len=0.045 r=0.009 up=40 tip
  sweep spike_b bone=upperarm.l at=0.0 offset=(0.0255,0.0367,0.024) dir=side on=pauldron.l material=plate
    seg len=0.024 r=0.012 up=50
    seg len=0.032 r=0.007 up=40 tip
  sweep spike_c bone=upperarm.l at=0.0 offset=(0.0255,0.0367,-0.024) dir=side on=pauldron.l material=plate
    seg len=0.024 r=0.012 up=50
    seg len=0.032 r=0.007 up=40 tip

  # SMARTWATCH — LEFT WRIST ONLY, matching the FPV arm asset (goblin-arm.glb,
  # F-arm.1). Outside the mirror block and pinned to forearm.l so no .r copy
  # is emitted. The band is a ring just above the wrist, proud of the flesh
  # (forearm shaft r 0.028, wrist blob r 0.030 -> 0.060 across) by 2 mm; the
  # body is a box snapped ONTO the band with on= so its landing needs no
  # offset guesswork, the screen a thinner box snapped onto the body.
  loft watchband bones=forearm.l..forearm.l material=band
    ring 0.88 w=0.062 d=0.062
    ring 0.92 w=0.064 d=0.064
    ring 0.96 w=0.062 d=0.062
    cap start=none end=none
  attach watchbody bone=forearm.l kind=box at=0.92 offset=(0,0,0.040) size=0.026 w=0.030 d=0.008 on=watchband material=band
  attach watchface bone=forearm.l kind=box at=0.92 offset=(0,0,0.048) size=0.020 w=0.024 d=0.002 on=watchbody material=screen

  # CHEST YOKE — the shallow plate between the pauldrons (owner, 2026-10-02: "football
  # pads", not a cuirass). The old breastplate was a full tube from belt to collar, which
  # is exactly what read as a metal shirt, and it was sized for a torso 10-30% fatter. The
  # trunk is now a DARK PAINTED SHIRT in goblin.blob, so the armour only has to be the
  # shoulder harness: this yoke closes the gap between the two pads over the upper chest
  # and leaves the shirt (and, next task, the crossed bandoliers) showing below it.
  #
  # OVERSIZED ON PURPOSE (owner, 2026-10-02: "slightly oversized, like an XL instead of
  # a small t-shirt"; the first fit read as a tight tee). So this is not flesh + a margin any
  # more: it is a LOOSE plate that stands off the chest and flares toward the hem, flesh x
  # 1.20 in width at the hem (the upper arms hang right beside the chest, so width is capped:
  # the arm's inner edge is x 0.0915 at y 0.878 and 0.0685 at y 0.931 (measure.md), and the
  # rings stay under it below the pad), x 1.28-1.36 in width higher up (hidden under the
  # pads, and above the shoulders there is no arm), and x 1.38 in DEPTH, where nothing limits
  # it. The hem drops to the bottom of the chest bone (y 0.837 m).
  #
  # Lofted on the chest bone alone (not a free ray: the bone is pitched 9 degrees and a
  # vertical ray drifts off it), t 0.00-0.97 of the 0.150 m bone. Flesh (measure.md, torso
  # cluster): chest t0 w 0.1516 d 0.1742, t0.25 0.1405 / 0.159, t0.50 0.124 / 0.140, t0.75
  # 0.1175 / 0.1348, t0.95 0.1124 / 0.131. Rings = flesh x factor / 1.30. History: the first
  # pass x1.10 had a scalloped edge and hairlines (the body grain's surface noise is a
  # millimetre or two deep and z-fought with a plate that close), x1.18 fixed that and read
  # as a tight tee, this is the loose version.
  loft yoke bones=chest..chest frame=fwd material=iron
    ring 0.00 w=0.1399 d=0.1849 fwd=0.004
    ring 0.25 w=0.1319 d=0.1688 fwd=0.003
    ring 0.50 w=0.1221 d=0.1486 fwd=0.002
    ring 0.75 w=0.1193 d=0.1431
    ring 0.97 w=0.1176 d=0.1391
    cap start=none end=none

  # SHADES — ROUND LENSES WITH EAR HOOKS (owner, 2026-10-02: "round lens sunglasses and give them
  # ear hooks that go up to his ears"). They replace the single wrap-around bar the first
  # version was; that read as a blindfold, and its justification (one sweep across both eyes, a
  # mirror pair would leave a gap over the nose) is answered here by an explicit nose bridge.
  #
  # Where things are (metres, measure.md "HEAD"): the skull bone at t0.45 is (0, 1.2239, 0.0037).
  # The painted eyes sit at about x +/-0.045 and y 1.229 (the old bar's height, eyeballed in the
  # lab because the face sheet's v does not run linearly down the skull). At y 1.24 the face's
  # front surface is z 0.094 at x 0.045 and falls away to 0.0685 at x 0.07, while the nose
  # bridge on the centreline is z 0.113: so a FLAT lens at z 0.122 clears the nose bridge by
  # 9 mm and floats 28 mm off the cheek, which is what sunglasses on a hooked nose do. The head's
  # half-width at y 1.24 is 0.0505 at z 0.09, 0.075 at z 0.06, 0.086 at z 0.03.
  #
  # LENS: a 16-sided disc (`sides=16`; the kit's `style chunky` is 8-sided, which would be a
  # stop sign), 56 mm across (0.0431 of height) and 8 mm thick, a free ray along +z from
  # (0.045, 1.229, 0.122): offset from skull t0.45 = (0.045, 0.0052, 0.1183) m /1.30 = (0.0346,
  # 0.004, 0.091). Material `glass`, so kit-overlay's emissive keeps the goblin's red eyes
  # reading through the lens (see its docstring). Mirrored, so both lenses.
  #
  # TEMPLE ARM: a sweep from the lens's outer edge (x 0.073) aimed back and OUTWARD, because
  # the head is 0.075-0.086 half-wide behind the cheek and a straight-back arm would run
  # into it. `dir=back yaw=-16` (a sweep takes no explicit vector, and a NEGATIVE yaw turns a
  # back-pointing sweep toward +x; WAM flips yaw under mirroring) with a 6 degree rise: tan(16) = 0.28 gains 28 mm of x over 0.1 m (x 0.090 at z 0.06, 0.099
  # at z 0.03, both >= 8 mm clear of the head), then a short bend UP (65 degrees, 20 mm) lifts it
  # from y 1.239 to the ear base at y ~1.26 (the ear root is at skull t0.58, offset
  # (0.086, 0.006, -0.006)), and a last small bend back hooks behind the ear. Radius 4.5 mm.
  mirror
    loft lens bone=skull at=0.45 offset=(0.0346,0.004,0.091) dir=fwd len=0.0062 frame=up sides=16 material=glass
      ring 0.00 w=0.0431 d=0.0431
      ring 1.00 w=0.0431 d=0.0431
      cap start=flat end=flat
    sweep temple bone=skull at=0.45 offset=(0.0562,0.004,0.091) dir=back yaw=-16 sides=8 material=black
      seg len=0.075 r=0.0035 up=6
      seg len=0.016 r=0.0032 up=65
      seg len=0.010 r=0.0030 back=35 tip
  end
  # NOSE BRIDGE — the one piece that spans the centreline: 30 mm between the lenses' inner
  # edges (x +/-0.017), 6 mm above the lens centre and so 10 mm clear of the nose.
  loft bridge bone=skull at=0.45 offset=(-0.0115,0.012,0.091) dir=side len=0.0230 sides=8 material=black
    ring 0.00 w=0.0058 d=0.0058
    ring 1.00 w=0.0058 d=0.0058
    cap start=flat end=flat

  # UTILITY BELT — rebuilt 2026-10-02 against the phase-1 body (owner: "some
  # kind of utility belt around the waist"; the old belt was sized for a gut
  # 11% wider and 27% deeper, and its buckle floated 5 cm off the new belly).
  #
  # It sits ON THE PAINT SEAM: the pants end and the shirt starts where the
  # pelvis and spine1 bars meet (y 0.705 m at rest, see goblin.blob), so the belt
  # is what a viewer reads as the join. Lofted along the hips..spine1 chain
  # (0.105 + 0.135 = 0.240 m, root at y 0.60), rings at y 0.679 / 0.701 / 0.722.
  # WAM FOLDED THAT: a loft spanning the hips->spine1 bend (13 deg) with rings this
  # wide doubles back ("missing faces"). So the belt is lofted on the hips bone alone,
  # rings at hips t0.66 / 0.84 / 1.00 = y 0.669 / 0.688 / 0.705 m: a 36 mm belt that
  # ends exactly on the paint seam. Ring sizes are the same flesh x 1.10 numbers.
  #
  # Flesh there (metres, measure.md): pelvis t0.75 w 0.1573 d 0.1761 with the
  # section's centre 0.0137 forward of the bone axis (the gut is in front, the
  # back is flat); pelvis t1.0 / spine1 t0 w 0.1618 d 0.1807, centre +0.0131;
  # spine1 t0.15 w ~0.158 d ~0.178, centre +0.006. THE WIDTH IS NOT THE TORSO'S: at belt
  # height the widest flesh is the TOPS OF THE THIGHS (hip joints at x 0.058, r 0.046),
  # so the whole-body width is 0.2088-0.2101 m (measure.md, "WHOLE BODY"), not the
  # torso cluster's 0.1618. The first belt used the torso number and its side vertices
  # sat 12 mm inside the thigh tops. Width = 0.209 x 1.10 / 1.30 = 0.1775; depth is
  # still the torso's (nothing else reaches front or back). Every ring is flesh x 1.10 / 1.30
  # with `fwd` carrying the centre offset: 10% is the facet margin for `style
  # chunky` plus a hair of belt thickness (facet midpoints land ~1-2 mm proud,
  # the vertices ~8 mm), thin on purpose, because a belt that stands off the
  # body reads as a hoop. The asymmetry matters: centred on the bone axis the
  # belt would float at the back and cut into the gut.
  loft belt bones=hips..hips material=leather
    ring 0.66 w=0.1775 d=0.1490 fwd=0.0105
    ring 0.84 w=0.1775 d=0.1529 fwd=0.0101
    ring 1.00 w=0.1775 d=0.1529 fwd=0.0101
    cap start=none end=none

  # BUCKLE — brass, front centre. NOT `on=belt` (it sinks the origin in by an
  # inset and buried the old buckle). Hand-placed: hips t0.95 is y 0.70 m, and the
  # belt's front vertex is 0.1117 m forward there, so the box (0.026 m deep)
  # is centred at z 0.1227 m = 0.0944 of height, its front face standing 11 mm
  # proud of the belt and its back face 2 mm inside it.
  attach buckle bone=hips kind=box at=0.95 offset=(0,0,0.0944) size=0.030 w=0.044 d=0.020 material=brass

  # POUCHES — at the belt's FRONT CORNERS, not at the hip: the hanging forearm
  # owns the flank (the soldier kit's measured lesson; the goblin's forearm is at
  # x 0.14-0.16 m and the hand at 0.144), so anything at x >= 0.10 m is swallowed
  # or collides in the swing. The corner is where the belt ellipse passes
  # x 0.070 m, z 0.090 m (0.0131 + 0.0969 * sqrt(1 - (0.070/0.1152)^2), the belt ellipse is
  # now 0.230 m wide); a box is centred 7 mm outside that so 2 mm of it is tucked into
  # the belt, hanging from the belt line (hips t0.81 = y 0.685 m). Boxes are 8 vertices each.
  mirror
    attach pouch bone=hips kind=box at=0.81 offset=(0.0538,0,0.0747) size=0.034 w=0.030 d=0.018 material=leather
  end

  # BACK POUCH — one, centred, on the flat back. The belt's back vertex is at
  # z -0.0863 m (0.0131 - 0.0994); the box is centred 11 mm behind that.
  attach backpouch bone=hips kind=box at=0.81 offset=(0,0,-0.0746) size=0.032 w=0.038 d=0.018 material=leather

  # NO FAULD, CLEAVER OR BUCKLER (owner, 2026-10-02). The pelvic skirt clipped
  # both thighs and the gut and is gone; the trousers are paint in goblin.blob.
  # The cleaver and the buckler were retired by the goblin refinement spec
  # (owner decision 2, 2026-10-01): phase 3 gives the goblin the player's
  # weapons through the held-prop system, so there is no weapon in this kit.

textures
  texture boot base=#2c2a28
    streaks along=v amount=0.07
    noise scale=0.14 amount=0.08
    ao amount=0.22
  texture webbing base=#3b3f42
    noise scale=0.12 amount=0.10
    ao amount=0.18
  texture plate base=#6e7378
    gradient axis=v from=-0.12 to=0.18
    noise scale=0.10 amount=0.06
    ao amount=0.18
  texture iron base=#6e7378
    gradient axis=v from=-0.12 to=0.18   # painted top-light on the plate
    noise scale=0.10 amount=0.06         # hammered, not stamped
    ao amount=0.18
  texture leather base=#4a3526
    streaks along=v amount=0.09
    noise scale=0.16 amount=0.08
    ao amount=0.20
  texture brass base=#9c7a38
    gradient axis=v from=-0.10 to=0.22
    ao amount=0.12
